  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1054 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nRidging continues to dominate the eastern half of the country, while \ntroughing digs into the Great Basin this evening. Southwesterly flow \nahead of this trough continues to stream anomalously rich moisture \ninto eastern Utah and western Colorado, although drier air is mixing \naloft. Even with the drying aloft, PWATs remain well above normal \nand are expected to continue to do so through Thursday. Broad \ndynamic lift, courtesy of a jet streak aloft and the close low at \nthe base of the approaching trough, will keep widespread \nprecipitation in the forecast, with chances for a stray thunderstorm \nor two. Minor accumulations of snow will be possible at elevations \nabove 10k through the period.\n\nAs the low swings across the Four Corners Wednesday morning, colder \nair will work in aloft. This will increase instability and start to \nlower snow levels a bit, resulting in the best chances for \naccumulating snowfall. Models remain zealous as far as snow totals \nare concerned, likely not accounting for the very warm antecedent \nconditions and still warm ground temperatures. A couple of inches at \nelevations above 9500 feet will be possible, and could lead to some \nslushy, messy commutes over mountain passes on Wednesday morning. \nHave continued to hold off on issuing any winter highlights, but \nwill reevaluate the need for them with the next forecast package. \n\nThe low finally tracks into the Plains Thursday, though wrap-around \nmoisture will keep showers going through Thursday afternoon for \nnorthern portions of the area. As skies clear, conditions are set \nfor good radiational cooling. Northern valleys will likely see lows \ndrop below freezing, so stay tuned for the potential for freeze \nproducts. \n\nAbundant cloud cover and showers, as well as the much cooler airmass \nushered in by the approaching trough, will keep temperatures well \nbelow normal through Thursday. Highs on Wednesday are currently \nforecast to run a good 10-15 degrees below mid-October normals. \nThings will certainly feel fall-like across the Western Slope. \nTemperatures start to rebound by the weekend as high pressure tries \nto build in from the west, reaching near-normal by Sunday.",
